1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,085
In a single year, one man was paid
about five hundred and fifty million

2
00:00:04,151 --> 00:00:08,466
dollars. He built an empire out of the
kind of debt the rest of Wall Street

3
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:11,121
dismissed as garbage. Then he went to prison.

4
00:00:11,188 --> 00:00:15,330
Decades later, a president
erased the punishment with a stroke of a pen.

5
00:00:15,397 --> 00:00:17,525
So what was Michael Milken, really? A

6
00:00:17,592 --> 00:00:20,526
genius the system
feared, or the face of its greed?

7
00:00:22,276 --> 00:00:26,006
There are a handful of people the world
can never quite make up its mind

8
00:00:26,072 --> 00:00:27,989
about. Michael Milken is one of them.

9
00:00:28,939 --> 00:00:33,546
Ask the people who admire him, and you will
hear about a misunderstood genius. The

10
00:00:33,613 --> 00:00:37,995
man who pried open the doors of capital
for companies the big banks had locked

11
00:00:38,062 --> 00:00:42,669
out. Who financed jobs and growth and whole
industries that did not yet exist. Who

12
00:00:42,736 --> 00:00:45,208
was then hunted down and made an example of,

13
00:00:45,275 --> 00:00:47,747
they say, for the crime of winning too much.

14
00:00:48,697 --> 00:00:52,938
Ask the people who can't stand
him, and you will hear the exact opposite.

15
00:00:53,004 --> 00:00:57,129
They will tell you he is the clearest
symbol the 1980s ever produced, a

16
00:00:57,195 --> 00:00:59,403
decade of greed handed a human face. A

17
00:00:59,469 --> 00:01:02,722
man who bent the rules of the market
until they snapped,

18
00:01:02,789 --> 00:01:06,797
paid himself more than
anyone in history, and then strolled away with

19
00:01:06,864 --> 00:01:10,291
a presidential pardon and most of his
fortune still intact.

20
00:01:11,241 --> 00:01:12,727
Here is the strange part.

21
00:01:12,793 --> 00:01:16,953
Almost everything in both of those
portraits is true at the same time.

22
00:01:17,903 --> 00:01:21,832
This is the story of how a kid
from the suburbs of Los Angeles,

23
00:01:21,898 --> 00:01:25,141
working at a desk most
Americans had never heard of,

24
00:01:25,208 --> 00:01:29,323
turned himself into the most powerful
financier in the country. In

25
00:01:29,390 --> 00:01:33,693
one year, he was paid more than
entire corporations earned in profit.

26
00:01:34,643 --> 00:01:37,447
It is the story of the idea that made him. A

27
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:42,421
simple, almost obvious insight about
debt that an entire industry had somehow

28
00:01:42,488 --> 00:01:47,650
walked straight past. It is also the story
of the line he eventually crossed, the

29
00:01:47,717 --> 00:01:51,222
place where that genius
curdled into six felony counts.

30
00:01:52,172 --> 00:01:56,144
And it is the story of a question that
has never really been settled.

31
00:01:56,210 --> 00:01:59,549
Not by the courts, not by his money,
not even by a pardon.

32
00:01:59,615 --> 00:02:03,645
Was Michael Milken a builder the system
punished for being too good at

33
00:02:03,711 --> 00:02:08,086
the game? Or a criminal the system was
simply rich enough to forgive? By the

34
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:11,491
end of this, you are
going to have to decide for yourself.

35
00:02:12,441 --> 00:02:15,994
To understand the man,
you first have to understand the idea,

36
00:02:16,061 --> 00:02:20,197
because the idea came first, and almost
everything else grew out of it.

37
00:02:21,947 --> 00:02:24,803
Picture the bond market in the early 1970s. A

38
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:29,440
bond is just a loan. A company borrows
money from investors and promises

39
00:02:29,507 --> 00:02:32,110
to pay it back, with interest, over time.

40
00:02:32,176 --> 00:02:36,874
Every bond gets a grade, like a student,
from the big rating agencies. The

41
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:40,559
safe, established blue-chip
companies earn the top marks.

42
00:02:40,625 --> 00:02:44,053
Investment grade. Their debt
is treated like gold, and

43
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:45,770
everyone wants to hold it.

44
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,439
Below a certain line sit
all the other bonds. The

45
00:02:49,506 --> 00:02:52,003
debt of smaller companies, younger companies,

46
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:55,399
companies that had stumbled
once and still carried the scar.

47
00:02:55,466 --> 00:02:56,964
Below investment grade. The

48
00:02:57,031 --> 00:02:59,306
market had a colder word for those. Junk.

49
00:03:00,256 --> 00:03:02,166
And the word did real damage.

50
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:05,723
Calling a company's debt
junk was not just an insult,

51
00:03:05,790 --> 00:03:07,634
it was closer to a sentence.

52
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:11,125
It told every cautious investor,
every pension fund,

53
00:03:11,192 --> 00:03:13,431
every bank, to stay well away. The

54
00:03:13,498 --> 00:03:15,276
label became the reality. A

55
00:03:15,343 --> 00:03:18,570
company branded junk could
not raise money, and a

56
00:03:18,636 --> 00:03:22,324
company that cannot raise
money struggles to grow, which

57
00:03:22,391 --> 00:03:25,223
makes it look even riskier than before. The

58
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:26,541
fear fed on itself.

59
00:03:27,491 --> 00:03:30,837
The rule on Wall Street was
practically carved in stone.

60
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:33,173
Respectable money does not touch junk.

61
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:36,944
It is too risky. Those companies
default, you lose your shirt,

62
00:03:37,011 --> 00:03:41,671
end of conversation. So the capital that
growing businesses needed most simply

63
00:03:41,737 --> 00:03:43,350
was not there for them. The

64
00:03:43,417 --> 00:03:47,599
door was shut, with a polite little
sign on it that said: not for you.

65
00:03:48,549 --> 00:03:51,304
So the rule carried a quiet
cruelty inside it. The

66
00:03:51,371 --> 00:03:54,622
biggest, safest companies, the ones that
needed help least,

67
00:03:54,689 --> 00:03:58,271
could borrow all day long at the cheapest
rates in the world. The

68
00:03:58,337 --> 00:04:02,195
smaller, hungrier companies, the ones a loan
might actually transform,

69
00:04:02,262 --> 00:04:04,190
were left standing out in the cold.

70
00:04:04,257 --> 00:04:07,233
Money flowed toward the people who
already had it, and

71
00:04:07,299 --> 00:04:09,393
away from the people who did not. That

72
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:13,703
was simply how the system worked, and almost
nobody thought to ask whether it

73
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:14,927
had to work that way.

74
00:04:15,877 --> 00:04:20,394
Milken studied this same despised
corner of the market as a young man, and

75
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:24,733
he noticed something everyone else
had filed away as obvious and never

76
00:04:24,799 --> 00:04:29,560
bothered to question. He pointed to research
suggesting that a basket of these

77
00:04:29,627 --> 00:04:32,190
low-rated bonds, held together as a group,

78
00:04:32,257 --> 00:04:36,896
actually paid investors more than
enough to cover the handful that went bad.

79
00:04:36,962 --> 00:04:38,915
Yes, some would default. But the

80
00:04:38,982 --> 00:04:42,827
high interest on all the rest
more than made up the difference.

81
00:04:42,894 --> 00:04:46,434
Priced for fear, the whole
category was quietly a bargain.

82
00:04:47,384 --> 00:04:49,988
Sit with how large that thought is. The

83
00:04:50,054 --> 00:04:53,726
entire market had agreed,
without really checking, that

84
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:55,862
junk was something to run from.

85
00:04:55,929 --> 00:04:59,935
Milken's claim was that the label
itself was wrong. That the

86
00:05:00,001 --> 00:05:04,942
fear was overpriced. And that buried
inside all that fear was an enormous,

87
00:05:05,008 --> 00:05:08,747
untapped opportunity,
just sitting there in plain sight.

88
00:05:09,697 --> 00:05:14,385
Then he took the second step, the one that
turned a clever observation into an

89
00:05:14,452 --> 00:05:18,479
empire. If these bonds were a good
deal for the people buying them,

90
00:05:18,546 --> 00:05:21,671
he could do something nobody
else was willing to do.

91
00:05:21,738 --> 00:05:25,765
He could help the shut-out
companies, the strivers and the upstarts

92
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:30,279
the blue-chip banks ignored,
raise real money by issuing exactly this kind

93
00:05:30,346 --> 00:05:34,914
of high-yield debt. He could open the
door that had been closed for so long.

94
00:05:35,864 --> 00:05:39,267
What did that actually
mean for an ordinary company? A

95
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:44,059
cable startup, a fledgling airline,
a manufacturer with a solid plan and no

96
00:05:44,126 --> 00:05:49,041
pedigree, the kind of business a traditional
bank would wave away at the door.

97
00:05:49,108 --> 00:05:53,708
Through Milken, that company could
suddenly borrow the money to build, to

98
00:05:53,774 --> 00:05:56,925
hire, to grow. For the people who
believed in him,

99
00:05:56,992 --> 00:05:58,567
this was the whole point.

100
00:05:58,634 --> 00:06:00,461
He was not gaming the system.

101
00:06:00,528 --> 00:06:05,632
He was widening it, letting in the strivers
the old club had kept out on purpose.

102
00:06:06,582 --> 00:06:10,694
He built the operation inside an investment
bank called Drexel Burnham

103
00:06:10,761 --> 00:06:13,346
Lambert. Not one of the white-shoe giants. A

104
00:06:13,412 --> 00:06:18,053
scrappier, second-tier firm, the kind that
had to try harder to get noticed. In

105
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:22,408
time he moved the whole high-yield
machine clear across the country, from

106
00:06:22,475 --> 00:06:25,177
New York out to Beverly Hills, California, and

107
00:06:25,244 --> 00:06:28,181
ran it from a now-legendary
X-shaped trading desk,

108
00:06:28,248 --> 00:06:33,006
often arriving before dawn so he would be
awake when the New York markets opened.

109
00:06:33,956 --> 00:06:36,578
That distance was its own kind of statement.

110
00:06:36,644 --> 00:06:40,100
He was not sitting at the center of the old
establishment,

111
00:06:40,167 --> 00:06:41,954
waiting politely to be let in.

112
00:06:42,021 --> 00:06:45,298
He was building a rival one,
three thousand miles away,

113
00:06:45,364 --> 00:06:48,999
around an idea the rest of Wall
Street had been too proud, or

114
00:06:49,065 --> 00:06:50,674
too frightened, to pick up.

115
00:06:51,624 --> 00:06:55,466
For a good while, the idea
worked the way only the best ideas do.

116
00:06:55,533 --> 00:06:59,138
Companies that could never have
gotten a loan suddenly could.

117
00:06:59,205 --> 00:07:01,214
Some of them grew into giants. The

118
00:07:01,281 --> 00:07:05,832
high-yield bonds Milken arranged helped
finance a wave of new businesses that

119
00:07:05,899 --> 00:07:09,091
went on to reshape how
Americans lived and worked. The

120
00:07:09,157 --> 00:07:12,290
door was open, and the money
came pouring through it.

121
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:16,148
If the story had stopped at that exact moment,

122
00:07:16,215 --> 00:07:19,944
Milken would be remembered as a pure
financial visionary, a

123
00:07:20,011 --> 00:07:23,804
man who found money where everyone
else saw only risk. But a

124
00:07:23,871 --> 00:07:27,411
tool this powerful does not
stay in one set of hands, or

125
00:07:27,478 --> 00:07:29,627
get used for only one purpose. The

126
00:07:29,694 --> 00:07:35,067
same machine that funded the upstarts could
fund something else entirely. And it did.

127
00:07:36,017 --> 00:07:38,955
What Milken built next went
well beyond a business.

128
00:07:39,022 --> 00:07:43,170
It was closer to a kingdom, and for
most of the 1980s he sat squarely at

129
00:07:43,237 --> 00:07:44,237
the center of it.

130
00:07:45,987 --> 00:07:49,558
The high-yield market he had
championed exploded. Over the

131
00:07:49,625 --> 00:07:54,797
decade it swelled from a forgotten corner
of finance into a market worth well over a

132
00:07:54,864 --> 00:07:58,558
hundred billion dollars, and Milken
was its undisputed king.

133
00:07:58,625 --> 00:08:02,196
If you wanted to raise serious
money outside the old club,

134
00:08:02,263 --> 00:08:06,327
there was one road, and it ran
through Beverly Hills, through him.

135
00:08:07,277 --> 00:08:11,765
This is where the story stops being only
about plucky underdogs getting a fair

136
00:08:11,832 --> 00:08:16,666
shot. Because that same flood of money
became the fuel for the defining drama of the

137
00:08:16,732 --> 00:08:18,516
decade. The corporate takeover.

138
00:08:19,466 --> 00:08:22,975
In the old world,
if you wanted to swallow a giant company,

139
00:08:23,042 --> 00:08:25,362
you had to already be a giant yourself.

140
00:08:25,428 --> 00:08:27,808
You needed cash you simply did not have.

141
00:08:27,874 --> 00:08:29,659
Milken changed the arithmetic.

142
00:08:29,725 --> 00:08:34,365
He could raise vast sums of debt,
fast, and hand a relatively small player the

143
00:08:34,431 --> 00:08:37,049
firepower to go after a much larger one. The

144
00:08:37,115 --> 00:08:39,970
hunter no longer had to be
bigger than its prey.

145
00:08:40,037 --> 00:08:43,011
It just had to be able to get
Milken on the phone.

146
00:08:43,961 --> 00:08:48,571
His firm even perfected a single
sheet of paper that could put a chill through

147
00:08:48,637 --> 00:08:50,115
any boardroom in America.

148
00:08:50,182 --> 00:08:53,609
It was a letter stating that
Drexel was, in its own words,

149
00:08:53,676 --> 00:08:57,518
"highly confident" it could
raise the money for a given takeover.

150
00:08:57,584 --> 00:08:59,535
Not a guarantee. Just confidence.

151
00:08:59,601 --> 00:09:03,738
Yet coming from Milken, the market
treated that letter as good as cash

152
00:09:03,805 --> 00:09:07,706
in hand. A corporate raider could
wave it at a sleeping giant, and

153
00:09:07,772 --> 00:09:11,732
suddenly no company in the country
felt too big to become a target.

154
00:09:12,682 --> 00:09:17,030
You have to understand how that
felt to the people running America's biggest

155
00:09:17,097 --> 00:09:21,445
companies. For generations, the largest
corporations had seemed untouchable,

156
00:09:21,512 --> 00:09:24,945
too vast and too
expensive for anyone to seriously threaten.

157
00:09:25,011 --> 00:09:27,929
Now a far smaller rival,
armed with Milken's money,

158
00:09:27,996 --> 00:09:30,113
could come for them almost overnight.

159
00:09:30,179 --> 00:09:34,642
Chief executives watched their own
stock the way you watch a storm rolling in.

160
00:09:34,709 --> 00:09:36,654
Milken had not just opened a door.

161
00:09:36,721 --> 00:09:39,524
He had handed out
keys to the whole neighborhood.

162
00:09:40,474 --> 00:09:44,875
Once a year, the powerful and the ambitious
flew out to California to gather

163
00:09:44,942 --> 00:09:49,227
around him, to raise money and strike
deals on a scale the old Wall Street

164
00:09:49,293 --> 00:09:53,752
could barely picture. For a few
days, the true center of American finance was

165
00:09:53,819 --> 00:09:55,845
not some marble tower in Manhattan.

166
00:09:55,912 --> 00:09:59,097
It was wherever Michael Milken
happened to be standing.

167
00:10:00,047 --> 00:10:05,256
No single financier had ever loomed this
large over the American economy. And

168
00:10:05,323 --> 00:10:10,329
history tends to repeat one lesson
about anyone who climbs that high, that

169
00:10:10,396 --> 00:10:11,952
fast, and that visibly.

170
00:10:12,018 --> 00:10:15,604
Sooner or later, the climb
itself becomes the target.

171
00:10:16,554 --> 00:10:20,927
Supporters saw this as the market
doing exactly what a market is supposed

172
00:10:20,994 --> 00:10:24,109
to do. Shaking the dust off lazy,
bloated companies.

173
00:10:24,176 --> 00:10:28,669
Forcing complacent managers to either
perform or step aside for someone who

174
00:10:28,736 --> 00:10:31,252
would. Critics saw something far darker. A

175
00:10:31,318 --> 00:10:35,452
wave of debt-fueled raids that buried
great companies under mountains

176
00:10:35,519 --> 00:10:38,694
of borrowing,
broke some of them apart for scrap, and

177
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:42,954
cost ordinary people their jobs,
all to enrich a handful of financiers

178
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:44,398
standing in the middle.

179
00:10:44,465 --> 00:10:46,861
They were watching the very same events.

180
00:10:46,928 --> 00:10:50,223
They just could not agree on what
they were looking at.

181
00:10:51,173 --> 00:10:55,339
What made the fight so bitter
is that the stakes were real on both sides.

182
00:10:55,406 --> 00:10:57,802
Real companies were built with this money,

183
00:10:57,869 --> 00:11:00,665
companies that had been
frozen out for years. And

184
00:11:00,732 --> 00:11:04,099
real workers found
themselves on the wrong end of a buyout,

185
00:11:04,166 --> 00:11:08,503
told the factory was being sold or shut
to help pay down a mountain of fresh

186
00:11:08,569 --> 00:11:12,678
debt. The same engine that created
opportunity for some created wreckage

187
00:11:12,745 --> 00:11:17,082
for others. Where a person landed on Michael
Milken often came down to which

188
00:11:17,149 --> 00:11:20,059
end of that engine they
happened to be standing at.

189
00:11:21,009 --> 00:11:23,633
In the middle of all of it sat the money.

190
00:11:23,699 --> 00:11:28,498
Milken's personal fortune in these
years is almost hard to say out loud. In

191
00:11:28,565 --> 00:11:33,428
one single year, 1987, his pay was
reported at around five hundred and fifty

192
00:11:33,495 --> 00:11:35,863
million dollars. Not his firm's haul.

193
00:11:35,929 --> 00:11:40,665
His. At the time, it was the largest
annual sum anyone in America had ever

194
00:11:40,731 --> 00:11:42,139
been recorded earning.

195
00:11:43,089 --> 00:11:44,815
Put it in plain terms. In a

196
00:11:44,882 --> 00:11:49,868
single year, one financier was paid a sum that
most successful companies would

197
00:11:49,935 --> 00:11:51,341
need a decade to earn.

198
00:11:51,408 --> 00:11:53,325
Not a corporation. Not a bank.

199
00:11:53,392 --> 00:11:57,483
One person. Money like that does
not just buy houses and planes.

200
00:11:57,550 --> 00:12:01,130
It buys influence,
access, and the benefit of the doubt.

201
00:12:01,197 --> 00:12:04,265
It also paints a very
large target on your back.

202
00:12:05,215 --> 00:12:08,155
Try to actually hold that
number in your head. By

203
00:12:08,222 --> 00:12:12,903
some measures, he was personally earning
more than the entire companies he was

204
00:12:12,969 --> 00:12:17,650
financing. More, in a year, than
household-name corporations booked in profit.

205
00:12:17,717 --> 00:12:21,137
One man, at one desk, on the wrong
coast for Wall Street,

206
00:12:21,204 --> 00:12:24,024
had turned himself into a financial
superpower.

207
00:12:24,974 --> 00:12:29,338
Almost nobody outside his own operation
truly understood how it all fit

208
00:12:29,405 --> 00:12:32,846
together. The web of buyers
and sellers, the favors, the

209
00:12:32,913 --> 00:12:37,584
timing, the financing, lived largely
inside one brilliant head and the tight

210
00:12:37,651 --> 00:12:40,786
circle around it. That
was the engine of his power.

211
00:12:40,852 --> 00:12:45,093
It would also, before very long,
become the source of his danger. The

212
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:49,216
more indispensable he made
himself, the more there was for someone

213
00:12:49,283 --> 00:12:50,283
to investigate.

214
00:12:51,233 --> 00:12:54,839
Power on that scale attracts
two things at the same moment.

215
00:12:54,905 --> 00:12:56,678
Imitators, and investigators.

216
00:12:56,744 --> 00:13:01,511
Wall Street had never watched anyone
gather this much control over the flow of

217
00:13:01,578 --> 00:13:04,572
money this fast. To a lot of powerful
people, the

218
00:13:04,639 --> 00:13:08,123
rivals he had beaten, the executives
he had cornered, the

219
00:13:08,189 --> 00:13:11,489
regulators watching the takeover
wars from Washington,

220
00:13:11,556 --> 00:13:14,978
Milken no longer looked
like a hero flinging doors open.

221
00:13:15,045 --> 00:13:18,468
He looked like a man who had
simply become too powerful,

222
00:13:18,534 --> 00:13:21,651
operating inside a market
only he fully understood.

223
00:13:22,601 --> 00:13:25,331
When you are the one who
designed the game, and

224
00:13:25,398 --> 00:13:28,883
you are winning it by margins
nobody has ever posted before,

225
00:13:28,950 --> 00:13:31,216
people stop asking how you got so good.

226
00:13:31,282 --> 00:13:35,058
They start asking whether you are
playing it straight. And in the

227
00:13:35,125 --> 00:13:39,598
second half of the 1980s, the government
began asking that question out loud.

228
00:13:40,548 --> 00:13:44,615
What investigators suspected was
not that Milken had backed the wrong

229
00:13:44,681 --> 00:13:48,100
companies. It was that
somewhere in the blizzard of deals,

230
00:13:48,166 --> 00:13:51,643
he had stopped
bending the rules and started breaking them.

231
00:13:51,710 --> 00:13:54,657
Proving that would
take a witness from the inside,

232
00:13:54,724 --> 00:13:57,258
someone who had sat close enough to see. In

233
00:13:57,325 --> 00:13:59,682
1986, the government found exactly that.

234
00:14:00,632 --> 00:14:03,739
Every empire seems to have a single
thread that,

235
00:14:03,806 --> 00:14:06,912
once it is pulled,
unravels the whole thing. For

236
00:14:06,979 --> 00:14:09,827
Milken, that thread had a name. Ivan Boesky.

237
00:14:11,577 --> 00:14:14,344
Boesky was a famous Wall Street speculator, an

238
00:14:14,411 --> 00:14:18,501
arbitrageur who made his fortune
betting on corporate takeovers, the

239
00:14:18,567 --> 00:14:21,575
very deals Milken's money
helped set in motion. In

240
00:14:21,642 --> 00:14:25,551
1986, Boesky was caught in a securities-fraud
scandal of his own.

241
00:14:25,618 --> 00:14:29,107
Facing his own ruin,
he did what cornered men so often do.

242
00:14:29,173 --> 00:14:33,083
He cooperated. He started
handing the government other names. And

243
00:14:33,150 --> 00:14:36,097
one of the names he gave
them was Michael Milken.

244
00:14:37,047 --> 00:14:41,693
Boesky's cooperation pointed investigators
toward a tangle of dealings between the

245
00:14:41,759 --> 00:14:45,215
two men. Arrangements where, the government
would allege, the

246
00:14:45,282 --> 00:14:50,041
true ownership of stock had been hidden, where
favors were quietly traded, where the

247
00:14:50,108 --> 00:14:53,507
disclosures the law requires
had been quietly falsified. The

248
00:14:53,574 --> 00:14:56,236
genius of the open door,
prosecutors suspected,

249
00:14:56,303 --> 00:14:58,456
had a hidden room tucked in behind it.

250
00:14:59,406 --> 00:15:02,505
These were never going to be
simple cases to make. The

251
00:15:02,572 --> 00:15:05,498
deals at the heart of them
were fiendishly complex,

252
00:15:05,565 --> 00:15:09,467
layered through company after
company and account after account, the

253
00:15:09,534 --> 00:15:13,723
kind of thing that took trained
investigators years just to map out. That

254
00:15:13,790 --> 00:15:15,225
complexity cut both ways.

255
00:15:15,291 --> 00:15:18,046
It made real wrongdoing
genuinely hard to prove.

256
00:15:18,113 --> 00:15:22,818
It also made almost anything look suspicious
to outsiders who could not follow the

257
00:15:22,885 --> 00:15:24,205
trail through the maze.

258
00:15:25,155 --> 00:15:27,810
For two years the investigation
ground forward,

259
00:15:27,877 --> 00:15:32,001
one of the largest securities-fraud
probes the country had ever launched.

260
00:15:32,067 --> 00:15:34,496
Then, in 1989, it landed with full force. A

261
00:15:34,563 --> 00:15:38,856
federal grand jury handed up a
ninety-eight-count indictment against Michael

262
00:15:38,923 --> 00:15:39,923
Milken.

263
00:15:40,873 --> 00:15:45,732
Ninety-eight counts. Many of them were
brought under a law called RICO, a

264
00:15:45,799 --> 00:15:49,992
racketeering statute originally
written to dismantle the mafia,

265
00:15:50,059 --> 00:15:53,453
now aimed
squarely at a financier in Beverly Hills.

266
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:55,584
Racketeering. Securities fraud.

267
00:15:55,650 --> 00:15:57,514
Mail fraud. Insider trading.

268
00:15:57,581 --> 00:15:59,577
Stock parking. Tax violations.

269
00:15:59,644 --> 00:16:04,836
Prosecutors signaled they would try to strip
away well over a billion dollars.

270
00:16:05,786 --> 00:16:10,740
Hold on right here, because this is the exact
spot where the headlines blur the

271
00:16:10,806 --> 00:16:12,938
truth, and the difference matters.

272
00:16:13,005 --> 00:16:15,450
Those ninety-eight counts were charges.

273
00:16:15,517 --> 00:16:19,843
Accusations. They were not
findings, and they were not a verdict. The

274
00:16:19,910 --> 00:16:22,418
government had accused him of all of it.

275
00:16:22,485 --> 00:16:26,498
It had proven none of it,
because the case never reached a jury.

276
00:16:27,448 --> 00:16:31,139
The pressure on him in that
moment is hard to overstate. A

277
00:16:31,206 --> 00:16:35,598
trial under RICO could have meant
decades behind bars and the seizure

278
00:16:35,664 --> 00:16:37,701
of nearly everything he owned. A

279
00:16:37,768 --> 00:16:42,796
plea would cap the damage, but it would
force him to stand up in open court and

280
00:16:42,862 --> 00:16:45,217
admit, out loud, that he was a felon.

281
00:16:45,284 --> 00:16:49,739
Whatever that choice was, it was not
made from a position of strength.

282
00:16:50,689 --> 00:16:53,518
In April of 1990,
Michael Milken made a choice.

283
00:16:53,585 --> 00:16:55,873
He chose not to fight it out at trial.

284
00:16:55,940 --> 00:17:00,635
He pleaded guilty. But the precise
shape of that plea is the thing you have to

285
00:17:00,702 --> 00:17:04,675
get exactly right. He did not plead
guilty to ninety-eight counts.

286
00:17:04,742 --> 00:17:06,247
He pleaded guilty to six.

287
00:17:07,197 --> 00:17:10,630
Six felony counts.
Securities and reporting violations,

288
00:17:10,697 --> 00:17:15,067
conspiracy, mail fraud, and a tax count,
several of them tied to those

289
00:17:15,134 --> 00:17:17,693
concealed-ownership dealings with Boesky.

290
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:20,007
Now notice what is not on that list.

291
00:17:20,074 --> 00:17:22,571
He did not plead guilty to racketeering.

292
00:17:22,638 --> 00:17:27,008
He did not plead guilty to a standalone
charge of insider trading. The

293
00:17:27,074 --> 00:17:30,695
RICO count, and the other
ninety-two counts, were dropped.

294
00:17:31,645 --> 00:17:35,627
Why does that distinction still
matter, all these years later?

295
00:17:35,694 --> 00:17:39,291
Because almost everyone
half-remembers this story wrong.

296
00:17:39,357 --> 00:17:42,826
They picture a courtroom
showdown, a guilty verdict, a

297
00:17:42,893 --> 00:17:47,453
Wall Street villain branded a racketeer
and an inside trader by a jury.

298
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:48,933
None of that happened.

299
00:17:48,999 --> 00:17:51,247
There was no trial, and no verdict.

300
00:17:51,314 --> 00:17:54,269
He was charged with those things, yes. But the

301
00:17:54,335 --> 00:17:58,703
deal he actually made was an admission to six
specific felonies, and

302
00:17:58,770 --> 00:18:01,146
racketeering was not one of them. The

303
00:18:01,213 --> 00:18:04,617
towering rest of that
indictment quietly disappeared.

304
00:18:05,567 --> 00:18:08,195
That gap has fueled the argument ever since.

305
00:18:08,262 --> 00:18:11,427
Critics look at the six
counts and see real crimes, a

306
00:18:11,494 --> 00:18:13,943
flat admission that the king had cheated.

307
00:18:14,010 --> 00:18:17,713
His defenders look at the same
six counts and see the reverse.

308
00:18:17,780 --> 00:18:21,901
Proof, they argue, that after the biggest
securities investigation in

309
00:18:21,968 --> 00:18:25,850
the nation's history, with everything
imaginable on the line, the

310
00:18:25,917 --> 00:18:30,158
government could only get
Milken to admit to a narrow set of technical,

311
00:18:30,224 --> 00:18:34,167
regulatory offenses. Nothing remotely
like the racketeering empire

312
00:18:34,233 --> 00:18:36,264
the indictment had screamed about.

313
00:18:37,214 --> 00:18:39,826
The punishment, though, was entirely real. The

314
00:18:39,893 --> 00:18:43,414
financial penalty came to about
six hundred million dollars, a

315
00:18:43,481 --> 00:18:47,286
record at the time, split
between a fine of two hundred million and

316
00:18:47,353 --> 00:18:51,612
a four-hundred-million-dollar
fund to compensate those who had been harmed.

317
00:18:51,679 --> 00:18:55,995
He was barred, for the rest of his
life, from the securities industry he had

318
00:18:56,062 --> 00:18:57,595
just spent a decade ruling.

319
00:18:57,662 --> 00:19:00,388
He was sentenced to ten
years in federal prison.

320
00:19:01,338 --> 00:19:05,430
Ten years. The king of Wall Street
was going to prison for a decade.

321
00:19:05,496 --> 00:19:08,505
Except he wasn't. The sentence
was later cut down,

322
00:19:08,572 --> 00:19:13,265
after he cooperated with prosecutors in other
cases and earned credit for good

323
00:19:13,332 --> 00:19:16,942
behavior. In the end, Michael
Milken served about two years.

324
00:19:17,892 --> 00:19:20,624
To one side, two years
was a slap on the wrist,

325
00:19:20,691 --> 00:19:23,772
more proof that the powerful
never really pay. To the

326
00:19:23,839 --> 00:19:28,141
other, the punishment had been crushing
no matter how many months he spent

327
00:19:28,208 --> 00:19:29,603
inside. A record fine. A

328
00:19:29,669 --> 00:19:33,913
ruined name. Permanent exile from the only
work he had ever wanted to do.

329
00:19:33,980 --> 00:19:37,119
Even his sentence,
like everything else about the man,

330
00:19:37,186 --> 00:19:38,186
split the room.

331
00:19:39,136 --> 00:19:44,178
So the man who had earned five
hundred and fifty million dollars in a single

332
00:19:44,244 --> 00:19:48,822
year walked out of a minimum-security
prison camp in the early 1990s.

333
00:19:48,888 --> 00:19:50,945
Banned from his own industry. A

334
00:19:51,011 --> 00:19:55,854
convicted felon. His name welded
forever to the excesses of a decade. The

335
00:19:55,921 --> 00:20:00,630
story could have ended right
there, as a tidy little morality tale. The

336
00:20:00,697 --> 00:20:04,213
greedy man climbed too
high, and the greedy man fell.

337
00:20:05,163 --> 00:20:08,601
It did not end there. Because
almost the moment he was free,

338
00:20:08,667 --> 00:20:12,850
two things happened, one after the other, that
would scramble the verdict

339
00:20:12,916 --> 00:20:15,380
on Michael Milken for the rest of his life.

340
00:20:16,330 --> 00:20:19,332
The first was a death
sentence of a different kind.

341
00:20:19,398 --> 00:20:23,813
Soon after his release, Milken was
diagnosed with advanced prostate cancer.

342
00:20:23,880 --> 00:20:25,410
It had already spread. The

343
00:20:25,477 --> 00:20:29,891
prognosis he was reportedly given was
measured not in years, but in months.

344
00:20:31,641 --> 00:20:35,730
Here the story takes the turn that
makes it so genuinely hard to judge.

345
00:20:35,796 --> 00:20:39,942
Faced with his own mortality,
Milken threw his fortune and his ferocious

346
00:20:40,009 --> 00:20:41,794
energy straight at the disease.

347
00:20:41,861 --> 00:20:45,316
He founded what became the Prostate
Cancer Foundation, which

348
00:20:45,383 --> 00:20:49,874
grew into the largest private funder of
prostate cancer research in the world.

349
00:20:49,941 --> 00:20:52,648
He had already
created the Milken Institute, an

350
00:20:52,714 --> 00:20:55,709
economic think tank that
still carries his name. The

351
00:20:55,775 --> 00:21:00,037
man the government had branded the face
of greed reinvented himself as one

352
00:21:00,103 --> 00:21:03,961
of the most significant medical
philanthropists in America. And the

353
00:21:04,028 --> 00:21:06,965
cancer that was supposed to kill
him inside a year?

354
00:21:07,032 --> 00:21:08,932
Decades later, he is still alive.

355
00:21:09,882 --> 00:21:13,802
The scale of that second act is
genuinely hard to overstate. The

356
00:21:13,869 --> 00:21:18,341
same drive that had once cornered the bond
market was now aimed at cancer

357
00:21:18,407 --> 00:21:22,818
research, at the scientists who could
not get funded anywhere else. In a

358
00:21:22,885 --> 00:21:26,376
strange way, it was the junk-bond
insight all over again.

359
00:21:26,443 --> 00:21:29,689
Find the thing the establishment
has written off, and

360
00:21:29,756 --> 00:21:31,226
pour everything into it.

361
00:21:31,293 --> 00:21:34,968
Only this time, the payoff was
measured not in money, but in

362
00:21:35,035 --> 00:21:36,260
years of human life.

363
00:21:37,210 --> 00:21:39,850
So which one is the real Michael Milken? The

364
00:21:39,917 --> 00:21:44,357
felon who paid a record fine,
or the philanthropist whose money has helped

365
00:21:44,424 --> 00:21:46,044
extend countless lives? The

366
00:21:46,110 --> 00:21:48,450
honest answer is the uncomfortable one.

367
00:21:48,517 --> 00:21:50,797
He is both, at the same time. And that

368
00:21:50,864 --> 00:21:54,764
is exactly what makes the final
chapter detonate the way it does.

369
00:21:55,714 --> 00:21:58,841
Because the second thing
arrived in February of 2020.

370
00:21:58,908 --> 00:22:02,448
President Donald Trump granted
Michael Milken a full pardon.

371
00:22:03,398 --> 00:22:08,433
The White House statement praised his
decades of philanthropy and described

372
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:09,976
his offenses as novel.

373
00:22:10,043 --> 00:22:13,400
Technical and regulatory
violations, it said, of a

374
00:22:13,466 --> 00:22:17,427
kind that had rarely if ever
been charged as crimes before.

375
00:22:17,494 --> 00:22:22,394
It listed a long roster of powerful
people who had pushed for the pardon.

376
00:22:22,461 --> 00:22:24,139
Financiers. Media moguls.

377
00:22:24,206 --> 00:22:25,750
Political heavyweights.

378
00:22:25,817 --> 00:22:30,986
After thirty years, the establishment
had not merely forgiven Michael Milken.

379
00:22:31,053 --> 00:22:35,349
Parts of it had spent years actively
lobbying to clear his name.

380
00:22:36,299 --> 00:22:39,396
By then he was no longer
just a disgraced financier.

381
00:22:39,463 --> 00:22:43,632
He was a major philanthropist, a man who
convened presidents and chief

382
00:22:43,699 --> 00:22:48,106
executives, whose name sat on a respected
think tank and a leading medical

383
00:22:48,173 --> 00:22:51,448
charity. So the pardon did
not fall out of a clear sky.

384
00:22:51,515 --> 00:22:55,863
It was the result of a long, patient,
well-funded campaign to reshape how

385
00:22:55,930 --> 00:22:57,419
the world remembered him.

386
00:22:57,485 --> 00:23:01,952
Whether that campaign was a search for
justice or a quiet purchase of it is

387
00:23:02,019 --> 00:23:04,580
precisely the thing people cannot agree on.

388
00:23:05,530 --> 00:23:09,135
It is worth being very clear
about what a pardon is, and

389
00:23:09,202 --> 00:23:11,777
what it is not. A pardon is forgiveness.

390
00:23:11,844 --> 00:23:15,578
It is the government choosing to set
aside the punishment.

391
00:23:15,644 --> 00:23:18,541
What it is not is a declaration of innocence.

392
00:23:18,608 --> 00:23:20,797
It does not erase the guilty plea.

393
00:23:20,864 --> 00:23:23,954
It does not say the six
felonies never happened.

394
00:23:24,020 --> 00:23:28,076
Michael Milken pleaded
guilty, and in the eyes of the law, that

395
00:23:28,143 --> 00:23:30,010
remains true to this day. The

396
00:23:30,077 --> 00:23:31,686
pardon forgave the crime.

397
00:23:31,753 --> 00:23:32,976
It did not undo it.

398
00:23:33,926 --> 00:23:37,008
There was one more thing the pardon
never touched. The

399
00:23:37,074 --> 00:23:39,700
lifetime ban from the securities industry, the

400
00:23:39,766 --> 00:23:43,989
regulators' judgment that he could
never again work in the business he had

401
00:23:44,056 --> 00:23:46,396
transformed, stayed exactly where it was.

402
00:23:46,463 --> 00:23:47,832
Forgiven by a president.

403
00:23:47,899 --> 00:23:49,668
Still barred by the regulators.

404
00:23:49,735 --> 00:23:54,871
Even at the very end, the verdict on Michael
Milken came back split right down the middle.

405
00:23:55,821 --> 00:24:00,116
So you can start to see why he
is a man the world cannot settle on. To

406
00:24:00,183 --> 00:24:03,066
his defenders, the pardon
was simple justice. A

407
00:24:03,133 --> 00:24:06,998
long-overdue correction for a man who
had been scapegoated, who

408
00:24:07,065 --> 00:24:11,790
had paid his price many times over, and who
had spent the decades since doing

409
00:24:11,856 --> 00:24:16,397
extraordinary good. To his critics, the pardon
was the most fitting ending

410
00:24:16,463 --> 00:24:19,531
a story about money and power
could possibly have.

411
00:24:19,598 --> 00:24:22,420
Proof that in America, if you are rich enough,

412
00:24:22,487 --> 00:24:25,800
connected enough, and generous
enough for long enough,

413
00:24:25,867 --> 00:24:29,180
even a record-setting financial
conviction can, in the

414
00:24:29,247 --> 00:24:31,394
end, simply be wiped off the board.

415
00:24:32,344 --> 00:24:35,468
Two opposite readings of the very
same act of mercy. The

416
00:24:35,535 --> 00:24:38,938
unsettling thing is that neither
one of them is simply wrong.

417
00:24:39,888 --> 00:24:44,571
In the end, the hardest thing about Michael
Milken is that he refuses to fit the

418
00:24:44,638 --> 00:24:46,511
shape we want to force him into.

419
00:24:46,577 --> 00:24:49,973
We like our financial villains to be
plainly villains, and

420
00:24:50,039 --> 00:24:51,854
our geniuses to come out clean.

421
00:24:51,921 --> 00:24:54,145
He is neither, and he is somehow both.

422
00:24:55,895 --> 00:25:00,198
He really did see something true that
an entire market had missed, and that

423
00:25:00,264 --> 00:25:04,395
insight really did open up
capital to companies that had been locked out

424
00:25:04,461 --> 00:25:07,330
of it. He really did plead
guilty to six felonies,

425
00:25:07,396 --> 00:25:11,584
pay a record penalty, and lose his
industry for the rest of his life. The

426
00:25:11,651 --> 00:25:14,691
builder and the convict
are the very same person, and

427
00:25:14,758 --> 00:25:18,372
you do not get to keep
only the half of him you happen to like.

428
00:25:19,322 --> 00:25:22,517
There is a strong temptation,
watching all this, to

429
00:25:22,584 --> 00:25:27,596
just pick the version that flatters what
you already believe and stop there. The

430
00:25:27,662 --> 00:25:30,168
hero punished by jealous regulators. The

431
00:25:30,235 --> 00:25:33,305
crook bailed out by his
powerful friends. But the

432
00:25:33,372 --> 00:25:37,945
facts are stubborn, and they refuse to line
up neatly behind either flag.

433
00:25:38,895 --> 00:25:42,222
Maybe that is the real lesson
buried in all of this.

434
00:25:42,289 --> 00:25:45,360
Not a lesson about
bonds, but a lesson about us.

435
00:25:45,427 --> 00:25:50,354
About how we decide, as a society, who
gets punished and who gets forgiven. A

436
00:25:50,421 --> 00:25:54,388
poorer man, with a smaller
fortune and fewer powerful friends,

437
00:25:54,455 --> 00:25:58,614
does not get a foundation,
a comeback, and a presidential pardon.

438
00:25:58,681 --> 00:26:00,280
Milken's genius was real.

439
00:26:00,347 --> 00:26:03,994
So was the machinery of wealth
and influence that, in the

440
00:26:04,061 --> 00:26:08,988
end, softened nearly every consequence
the system tried to bring down on him.

441
00:26:09,938 --> 00:26:14,690
Every part of this story has two
endings stacked on top of each other. A

442
00:26:14,757 --> 00:26:17,133
record fortune, and a record fine. A

443
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:20,368
ten-year sentence, and two
short years served. A

444
00:26:20,434 --> 00:26:23,207
lifetime ban, and a presidential pardon. A

445
00:26:23,273 --> 00:26:27,233
man told he had months to live,
still here decades later. At

446
00:26:27,300 --> 00:26:32,184
every single fork, the story refuses to hand
you the clean answer you were

447
00:26:32,251 --> 00:26:33,251
reaching for.

448
00:26:34,201 --> 00:26:37,345
So we are going to do
something we almost never do.

449
00:26:37,412 --> 00:26:40,063
We are going to leave the verdict with you.

450
00:26:40,130 --> 00:26:44,384
You have heard what he built, and you
have heard what he admitted to.

451
00:26:44,450 --> 00:26:48,088
You have heard about the record
fine, the lifetime ban, the

452
00:26:48,154 --> 00:26:52,963
cancer, the billions given away,
and the pardon that forgave the crime without

453
00:26:53,030 --> 00:26:54,030
ever erasing it.

